  p_Name_8cb035cd8380 := 'ExportableData';
  p_Version_8cb035cd8380 := '1.0.0';
  p_VersionSequence_8cb035cd8380 := 1;
  p_Title_8cb035cd8380 := 'Exportable Data';
  p_Description_8cb035cd8380 := 'Wrapper that makes one data element exportable. It renders its child unchanged and adds a small export icon in the element''s top-right corner. The icon opens a menu: **CSV** and **Excel** for every element, plus **PNG** when `kind="chart"`. Files are named `<label>_<YYYY-MM-DD>.<ext>`. The wrapper writes the files itself; generated code contains no export logic, only which element is exportable and which rows it displays.

#### ExportableData - wrap:
✅ A custom table: a raw `<table>` or an antd `Table`
✅ A custom chart: Chart.js, ApexCharts, D3, or a data-driven `<svg>`, whatever the spec calls it

❌ DO NOT wrap:
- `DataGrid`, `EntityDataGrid` or `SimpleChart` → they export on their own
- KPI tiles, a single record''s detail panel, a layout table, decorative SVG, a tiny inline sparkline → leave unwrapped with `{/* export-opt-out: <reason> */}` beside it
- Never guard it (`ExportableData ? … : …`) or alias it; it is always in `components` once listed as a dependency

**`rows` is the displayed data**, not the raw fetched records. Table: all rows after the user''s filters and sort, across all pages. Chart: wide rows, one per x-axis value with one column per series or measure. Percents are fractions (0.25), not strings.

**Wrap the sized container**, not a bare `<canvas>`: the wrapper''s div becomes the element''s parent, and a responsive chart sizes itself to its parent.
';
  p_Type_8cb035cd8380 := 'Widget';
  p_Status_8cb035cd8380 := 'Published';
  p_Specification_8cb035cd8380 := '{"name":"ExportableData","title":"Exportable Data","description":"Wrapper that makes one data element exportable. It renders its child unchanged and adds a small export icon in the element''s top-right corner. The icon opens a menu: **CSV** and **Excel** for every element, plus **PNG** when `kind=\"chart\"`. Files are named `<label>_<YYYY-MM-DD>.<ext>`. The wrapper writes the files itself; generated code contains no export logic, only which element is exportable and which rows it displays.\n\n#### ExportableData - wrap:\n✅ A custom table: a raw `<table>` or an antd `Table`\n✅ A custom chart: Chart.js, ApexCharts, D3, or a data-driven `<svg>`, whatever the spec calls it\n\n❌ DO NOT wrap:\n- `DataGrid`, `EntityDataGrid` or `SimpleChart` → they export on their own\n- KPI tiles, a single record''s detail panel, a layout table, decorative SVG, a tiny inline sparkline → leave unwrapped with `{/* export-opt-out: <reason> */}` beside it\n- Never guard it (`ExportableData ? … : …`) or alias it; it is always in `components` once listed as a dependency\n\n**`rows` is the displayed data**, not the raw fetched records. Table: all rows after the user''s filters and sort, across all pages. Chart: wide rows, one per x-axis value with one column per series or measure. Percents are fractions (0.25), not strings.\n\n**Wrap the sized container**, not a bare `<canvas>`: the wrapper''s div becomes the element''s parent, and a responsive chart sizes itself to its parent.\n","type":"widget","location":"embedded","functionalRequirements":"## Purpose\nGive every table and chart in an interactive component the same export affordance, the same formats and the same file quality, whatever code the LLM wrote around it.\n\n## Affordance\n- One icon button in the element''s top-right corner, over the element (no layout change to the child).\n- Subtle at rest, stronger on hover and keyboard focus, always visible on touch devices.\n- Opens a menu: **CSV**, **Excel**, and **PNG** (charts only), in that order.\n- Keyboard accessible: the button is focusable, Enter/Space opens the menu, arrow keys move, Escape closes. The button''s accessible name is \"Export <label>\".\n- While a file is being prepared (async `getRows`, image capture) the menu item shows a spinner; a failure shows a short message and logs the error. It never throws into the host component.\n\n## Data\n- Exports `rows` (or the result of `getRows()`) with `columns`, in column order. `getRows` may return `{ rows, note }`; the note becomes the file''s last row, for a caller whose rows are partial.\n- A table exports every row that passes the user''s filters, in the current sort, across all pages.\n- A chart exports what it draws, in wide rows.\n- Empty rows: the menu still opens; CSV and Excel produce a header-only file.\n\n## Formats\n| Format | Behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| CSV | UTF-8 with BOM, RFC 4180 quoting, CRLF line endings, header row of column labels. Dates as ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` (or ISO date-time when the value has a time). Cells starting with `=`, `+`, `-` or `@` are prefixed with `''` so spreadsheets do not run them as formulas. |\n| Excel | One `.xlsx` sheet named after the label (31-character limit). Typed cells: number, currency (`$#,##0.00`), percent (`0.0%`), date (`yyyy-mm-dd`, or with time), boolean. A filter on the header row and column widths fitted to content. (The community build of SheetJS writes no cell styles, so the header is not bold or frozen.) |\n| PNG | Charts only. Native export when the chart library offers one (Chart.js at 3× pixel ratio; ApexCharts at scale 3), otherwise an html2canvas capture of the wrapper''s subtree at 2×. Drawn on the background the element is shown on, so a dark theme''s labels stay readable. |\n\n- Filename: `<label>_<YYYY-MM-DD>.<ext>`, with characters outside letters, digits, space, `-` and `_` replaced by `_`.\n- Excel''s row limit (1,048,576) is enforced: larger exports are cut off, with a note row at the end.\n\n## Integration\n- Registry components (`DataGrid`, `EntityDataGrid`, `SimpleChart`) render it internally; generated code never wraps them.\n- When the host provides the export runtime context (MJ host releases after this component), the element registers with it so the host can list and drive element exports and the root PDF. Without that context it works on its own.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Save as List (later, depends on MJ List write paths).\n- Raw underlying records behind a summarized chart.\n- Whole-component PDF (host-owned).\n","technicalDesign":"## Structure\n```\n<div data-export-id={exportID} style={{ position: ''relative'', ...style }}>\n  {children}\n  <ExportMenu />   // absolutely positioned top-right, z-index above the child\n</div>\n```\n- The div is the only element added to the layout. A flex or grid item that is wrapped keeps its layout by moving its layout styles to `style`.\n- The menu is an antd `Dropdown` (renders in a portal, so a parent with `overflow: hidden` does not clip it). The trigger is a plain `<button>` so it works without antd styles loaded.\n- Nothing in the child is cloned or given extra props; the wrapper finds chart instances by searching its own subtree.\n\n## Data flow\n1. On a menu click, resolve rows: `await getRows()` when given, else `rows`, else `[]`. `getRows` may return `{ rows, note }` instead of an array; the note is written as the file''s last row (CSV and Excel). The host registration''s `GetRows` returns the rows only.\n2. Resolve columns: `columns`, else the keys of the first row as `string` columns (logs a `console.warn` with the `exportID`).\n3. Normalize each cell by column type (shared by CSV and Excel):\n   - `number` / `currency` / `percent`: `Number(value)` when finite, else empty.\n   - `date`: `Date` or parseable ISO string → `Date`; anything else is written as text.\n   - `boolean`: `true`/`false`.\n   - `string`: `String(value)`; `null`/`undefined` → empty.\n4. Write the file and download it through a temporary object URL and anchor, revoked right after.\n\nThe download goes through one internal `deliverFile(blob, fileName, utilities)` function. When the host provides `utilities.export.DownloadFile(blob, fileName)` (MJ host releases after this component; the name is provisional until MJ defines the service), it is used instead of the browser download.\n\n## Writers\n- **CSV**: hand-written (no library). BOM `﻿`, fields quoted when they contain `\"`, `,`, CR or LF, quotes doubled, formula-prefix guard on text cells.\n- **Excel**: the sheet is built cell by cell with typed cells (`t: ''n'' | ''b'' | ''s''` and `z` number formats), `!cols` widths and an `!autofilter` on the header row, then `XLSX.write(..., { type: ''array'', bookType: ''xlsx'' })`. Dates are written as Excel serial numbers computed from their calendar parts, so no time zone shifts them; a bare `YYYY-MM-DD` string stays on its day.\n\n## PNG\nIn order:\n1. `getImage()` when given.\n2. A Chart.js chart in the subtree: `Chart.getChart(canvas)`. Temporarily set `devicePixelRatio: 3`, `animation: false`, resize, `toBase64Image(''image/png'')`, then restore options and resize.\n3. An ApexCharts chart in the subtree (`.apexcharts-canvas`): find the instance through `ApexCharts.getChartByID` on the element''s id, then `dataURI({ scale: 3 })`.\n4. Fallback: `html2canvas(wrapperDiv, { scale: 2, backgroundColor: <background>, ignoreElements: the export button })`.\n\nThe image from steps 1–3 is drawn onto a canvas filled with `<background>`: the nearest opaque `background-color` from the wrapper up (translucent ones blended over it, white when there is none), resolved through a 1×1 canvas so any CSS colour syntax works. A dark theme''s light chart text would vanish on white. An opaque image is unchanged.\n\n## Runtime registration\nWhen the host provides `utilities.export.RegisterElement` (MJ host releases after this component; provisional name), register on mount and call the returned function on unmount:\n`{ ExportID, GetLabel, GetKind, GetRows, GetColumns, GetImage, Element }`, with stable callbacks that read the latest props through a ref (not data snapshots). Feature-detected; an absent service is not an error.\n\nThe export button sits in a `data-export-control=\"true\"` container, which the PNG capture ignores and a host print mode can hide.\n\n## Error handling\nEvery export path is wrapped in try/catch: the error is logged with the `exportID` and format, and the menu shows \"Export failed\". No error reaches the host component or its error boundary.\n\n## Libraries\n- `antd` 5.12.0: menu.\n- `xlsx` 0.18.5: Excel writer.\n- `html2canvas` 1.4.1: PNG fallback.\n","dataRequirements":{"mode":"views","description":"Loads no data. Exports the rows the caller passes in `rows`, or the rows `getRows` returns."},"typeDefinitions":{"ExportColumn":{"description":"One column of the exported file, in file order.","properties":{"field":{"type":"string","required":true,"description":"Key of the value in each row object"},"label":{"type":"string","required":false,"description":"Header text in the file. Defaults to `field`."},"type":{"type":"''string'' | ''number'' | ''currency'' | ''percent'' | ''date'' | ''boolean''","required":false,"description":"Cell type in Excel. `percent` values are fractions (0.25 is 25%). `date` accepts a Date or an ISO string. Defaults to ''string''."}}}},"properties":[{"name":"exportID","type":"string","description":"Identifier of this element, kebab-case and unique within the component. Write it as a string literal (e.g. \"revenue-by-month\"), never an expression.","required":true,"exampleValue":"\"revenue-by-month\""},{"name":"label","type":"string","description":"Human name of the element. Used in the menu tooltip and as the filename: `<label>_<YYYY-MM-DD>.<ext>`.","required":true,"exampleValue":"\"Revenue by Month\""},{"name":"kind","type":"''table'' | ''chart''","description":"''table'' offers CSV and Excel. ''chart'' also offers PNG.","required":true,"exampleValue":"\"chart\""},{"name":"rows","type":"Array<object>","description":"The data the element DISPLAYS, not the raw fetched records. Table: the rows after the user''s filters and sort, across all pages. Chart: wide rows, one per x-axis value (category, month or plotted point) with one column per series or measure; network and sankey charts give one row per link. Keep values typed: numbers as numbers, percents as fractions.","required":false},{"name":"getRows","type":"() => Array<object> | { rows: Array<object>, note?: string } | Promise<Array<object> | { rows: Array<object>, note?: string }>","description":"Called at export time instead of reading `rows`. For registry components whose rows are not all in memory (EntityDataGrid loads one page at a time). Return `{ rows, note }` to end the file with a note row, such as when the rows were capped. Generated components pass `rows`.","required":false},{"name":"columns","type":"Array<ExportColumn>","description":"The columns of the file, in order. Give every column a `type` so Excel cells are typed. If omitted, columns are taken from the keys of the first row as strings, and a warning is logged.","required":true,"exampleValue":"[{ \"field\": \"Month\", \"label\": \"Month\", \"type\": \"string\" }, { \"field\": \"Revenue\", \"label\": \"Revenue\", \"type\": \"currency\" }]"},{"name":"getImage","type":"() => Promise<string>","description":"Returns a PNG data URL of the element, which the wrapper draws on the background the element is shown on. Only for registry components that own their chart instance. When omitted, the wrapper finds a Chart.js or ApexCharts chart inside itself and uses that library''s own export, then falls back to a capture of its own subtree.","required":false},{"name":"placement","type":"''overlay'' | ''toolbar''","description":"Where the export button sits. ''overlay'' (default): over the element''s top-right corner, shown on hover or keyboard focus and always on touch screens; no space is reserved. ''toolbar'': flush with the top-right corner at the 32px control height and always shown, for a parent whose toolbar row (such as DataGrid''s search box) leaves 40px free on its right. Generated components leave it unset.","required":false,"defaultValue":"overlay"},{"name":"style","type":"object","description":"Style for the wrapper''s own div, which replaces the wrapped element in its parent''s layout. Move layout styles here (e.g. `{ flex: 1, minWidth: 0 }`) when the wrapped element is a flex or grid item. The wrapper always sets `position: relative`.","required":false},{"name":"children","type":"ReactNode","description":"The element to make exportable: a table, or a chart together with its sized container. Rendered unchanged.","required":true}],"events":[],"libraries":[{"name":"antd","version":"5.12.0","globalVariable":"antd"},{"name":"xlsx","version":"0.18.5","globalVariable":"XLSX"},{"name":"html2canvas","version":"1.4.1","globalVariable":"html2canvas"}],"dependencies":[],"code":"// ExportableData wraps one table or chart and gives it CSV, Excel and (charts only) PNG export.\n// MJ''s linter allows one top-level function per component file, so the helpers live inside it.\nfunction ExportableData({\n  exportID,\n  label,\n  kind = ''table'',\n  rows,\n  getRows,\n  columns,\n  getImage,\n  placement = ''overlay'',\n  style,\n  children,\n  utilities,\n  styles,\n  components,\n  callbacks,\n  savedUserSettings,\n  onSaveUserSettings\n}) {\n  const EXCEL_MAX_DATA_ROWS = 1048573; // Excel''s 1,048,576 rows, less the header, the truncation note and the caller''s note\n  const EXCEL_NUMBER_FORMATS = {\n    number: ''General'',\n    currency: ''$#,##0.00'',\n    percent: ''0.0%'',\n    date: ''yyyy-mm-dd'',\n    datetime: ''yyyy-mm-dd hh:mm:ss''\n  };\n  const NUMERIC_TYPES = [''number'', ''currency'', ''percent''];\n  const CAPTURE_MAX_ELEMENTS = 20000; // bounds the overflow scan on very large drawings\n  const DATE_ONLY_PATTERN = /^(\\d{4})-(\\d{2})-(\\d{2})$/;\n  const FORMULA_PREFIX_PATTERN = /^[=+\\-@\\t\\r]/;\n\n  /**\n   * Chart.js and ApexCharts as the element''s own code loaded them. They are not dependencies of this\n   * component (most elements need neither), so they are read as globals and may be absent.\n   */\n  function findChartLibraries() {\n    return {\n      chartJs: typeof Chart !== ''undefined'' ? Chart : undefined,\n      apexCharts: typeof ApexCharts !== ''undefined'' ? ApexCharts : undefined\n    };\n  }\n\n  /** `<label>_<YYYY-MM-DD>.<ext>`, with anything outside letters, digits, space, - and _ replaced by _. */\n  function buildFileName(label, extension, now) {\n    const date = now || new Date();\n    const stamp = `$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{date.getFullYear()}-$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{pad2(date.getMonth() + 1)}-$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{pad2(date.NOW())}`;\n    const base = String(label || ''export'').trim().replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9 _-]/g, ''_'') || ''export'';\n    return `$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{base}_$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{stamp}.$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{extension}`;\n  }\n\n  function pad2(value) {\n    return String(value).padStart(2, ''0'');\n  }\n\n  /** The declared columns, or (with a warning) the first row''s keys as text columns. */\n  function resolveColumns(columns, rows, exportID) {\n    if (Array.isArray(columns) && columns.length > 0) {\n      return columns\n        .filter(column => column && column.field)\n        .map(column => ({ field: column.field, label: column.label || column.field, type: column.type || ''string'' }));\n    }\n    console.warn(`[ExportableData] \"$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{exportID}\" has no columns; using the keys of the first row as text columns.`);\n    const first = Array.isArray(rows) && rows.length > 0 ? rows[0] : {};\n    return Object.keys(first || {}).map(field => ({ field, label: field, type: ''string'' }));\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * Splits a date value into the calendar parts the user sees. A bare `YYYY-MM-DD` string stays on its\n   * calendar day (it would otherwise parse as UTC midnight and shift a day west of Greenwich).\n   */\n  function toDateParts(value) {\n    if (typeof value === ''string'') {\n      const dateOnly = DATE_ONLY_PATTERN.exec(value.trim());\n      if (dateOnly) {\n        return { year: +dateOnly[1], month: +dateOnly[2], day: +dateOnly[3], hours: 0, minutes: 0, seconds: 0, hasTime: false };\n      }\n    }\n    const date = value instanceof Date ? value : typeof value === ''string'' || typeof value === ''number'' ? new Date(value) : null;\n    if (!date || isNaN(date.getTime())) return null;\n    const hasTime = date.getHours() !== 0 || date.getMinutes() !== 0 || date.getSeconds() !== 0;\n    return {\n      year: date.getFullYear(), month: date.getMonth() + 1, day: date.NOW(),\n      hours: date.getHours(), minutes: date.getMinutes(), seconds: date.getSeconds(), hasTime\n    };\n  }\n\n  /** Normalizes one value by column type. Kinds: number, date, boolean, string, empty. */\n  function toCell(value, type) {\n    if (value === null || value === undefined || value === '''') return { kind: ''empty'' };\n    if (NUMERIC_TYPES.includes(type)) {\n      const number = typeof value === ''number'' ? value : Number(value);\n      return Number.isFinite(number) ? { kind: ''number'', value: number } : { kind: ''empty'' };\n    }\n    if (type === ''date'') {\n      const parts = toDateParts(value);\n      return parts ? { kind: ''date'', value: parts } : { kind: ''string'', value: String(value) };\n    }\n    if (type === ''boolean'') {\n      if (typeof value === ''boolean'') return { kind: ''boolean'', value };\n      const text = String(value).toLowerCase();\n      if (text === ''true'' || text === ''1'' || text === ''yes'') return { kind: ''boolean'', value: true };\n      if (text === ''false'' || text === ''0'' || text === ''no'') return { kind: ''boolean'', value: false };\n      return { kind: ''string'', value: String(value) };\n    }\n    return { kind: ''string'', value: typeof value === ''object'' ? JSON.stringify(value) : String(value) };\n  }\n\n  function formatDateParts(parts) {\n    const day = `$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{parts.year}-$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{pad2(parts.month)}-$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{pad2(parts.day)}`;\n    return parts.hasTime ? `$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{day} $' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{pad2(parts.hours)}:$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{pad2(parts.minutes)}:$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{pad2(parts.seconds)}` : day;\n  }\n\n  /** Excel''s serial date: days since 1899-12-30, computed from calendar parts so no time zone shifts it. */\n  function toExcelSerial(parts) {\n    const utc = Date.UTC(parts.year, parts.month - 1, parts.day, parts.hours, parts.minutes, parts.seconds);\n    return (utc - Date.UTC(1899, 11, 30)) / 86400000;\n  }\n\n  /** Text cells that a spreadsheet would run as a formula get a leading apostrophe. */\n  function guardFormula(text) {\n    return FORMULA_PREFIX_PATTERN.test(text) ? `''$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{text}` : text;\n  }\n\n  function toCsvField(cell) {\n    let text;\n    if (cell.kind === ''empty'') text = '''';\n    else if (cell.kind === ''number'') text = String(cell.value);\n    else if (cell.kind === ''date'') text = formatDateParts(cell.value);\n    else if (cell.kind === ''boolean'') text = cell.value ? ''true'' : ''false'';\n    else text = guardFormula(cell.value);\n    return /[\",\\r\\n]/.test(text) ? `\"$' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{text.replace(/\"/g, ''\"\"'')}\"` : text;\n  }\n\n  /** RFC 4180 CSV with a UTF-8 BOM and CRLF line endings; `note`, when given, is the last line. */\n  function buildCsv(rows, columns, note) {\n    const header = columns.map(column => toCsvField({ kind: ''string'', value: column.label }));\n    const lines = [header.join('','')];\n    for (const row of rows || []) {\n      lines.push(columns.map(column => toCsvField(toCell(row ? row[column.field] : undefined, column.type))).join('',''));\n    }\n    if (note) lines.push(toCsvField({ kind: ''string'', value: note }));\n    return ''﻿'' + lines.join(''\\r\\n'') + ''\\r\\n'';\n  }\n\n  function toExcelCell(cell, type) {\n    if (cell.kind === ''number'') return { t: ''n'', v: cell.value, z: EXCEL_NUMBER_FORMATS[type] || ''General'' };\n    if (cell.kind === ''date'') {\n      return { t: ''n'', v: toExcelSerial(cell.value), z: cell.value.hasTime ? EXCEL_NUMBER_FORMATS.datetime : EXCEL_NUMBER_FORMATS.date };\n    }\n    if (cell.kind === ''boolean'') return { t: ''b'', v: cell.value };\n    if (cell.kind === ''string'') return { t: ''s'', v: cell.value };\n    return null;\n  }\n\n  /** Display width of a cell in characters, for fitting column widths. */\n  function cellWidth(cell) {\n    if (cell.kind === ''date'') return cell.value.hasTime ? 19 : 10;\n    if (cell.kind === ''number'') return String(cell.value).length + 4;\n    if (cell.kind === ''empty'') return 0;\n    return String(cell.value).length;\n  }\n\n  /** One typed sheet: header row with an autofilter, fitted column widths, note rows when cut off or given a `note`. */\n  function buildWorksheet(xlsx, rows, columns, note) {\n    const sheet = {};\n    const widths = columns.map(column => String(column.label).length);\n    const dataRows = (rows || []).slice(0, EXCEL_MAX_DATA_ROWS);\n    columns.forEach((column, c) => {\n      sheet[xlsx.utils.encode_cell({ r: 0, c })] = { t: ''s'', v: String(column.label) };\n    });\n    dataRows.forEach((row, index) => {\n      columns.forEach((column, c) => {\n        const cell = toCell(row ? row[column.field] : undefined, column.type);\n        const excelCell = toExcelCell(cell, column.type);\n        if (excelCell) sheet[xlsx.utils.encode_cell({ r: index + 1, c })] = excelCell;\n        widths[c] = Math.max(widths[c], cellWidth(cell));\n      });\n    });\n    const notes = [];\n    if ((rows || []).length > dataRows.length) notes.push(`Truncated: $' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{dataRows.length} of $' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{rows.length} rows fit in Excel.`);\n    if (note) notes.push(note);\n    notes.forEach((text, index) => {\n      sheet[xlsx.utils.encode_cell({ r: dataRows.length + 1 + index, c: 0 })] = { t: ''s'', v: text };\n    });\n    const lastRow = dataRows.length + notes.length;\n    const lastColumn = Math.max(columns.length - 1, 0);\n    sheet[''!ref''] = xlsx.utils.encode_range({ s: { r: 0, c: 0 }, e: { r: lastRow, c: lastColumn } });\n    sheet[''!autofilter''] = { ref: xlsx.utils.encode_range({ s: { r: 0, c: 0 }, e: { r: 0, c: lastColumn } }) };\n    sheet[''!cols''] = widths.map(width => ({ wch: Math.min(Math.max(width + 2, 8), 60) }));\n    return sheet;\n  }\n\n  /** Excel''s sheet name rules: at most 31 characters, none of : \\ / ? * [ ]. */\n  function toSheetName(label) {\n    return (String(label || ''Data'').replace(/[:\\\\/?*[\\]]/g, '' '').trim() || ''Data'').substring(0, 31);\n  }\n\n  function buildXlsxBlob(xlsx, rows, columns, label, note) {\n    const workbook = xlsx.utils.book_new();\n    xlsx.utils.book_append_sheet(workbook, buildWorksheet(xlsx, rows, columns, note), toSheetName(label));\n    const bytes = xlsx.write(workbook, { bookType: ''xlsx'', type: ''array'' });\n    return new Blob([bytes], { type: ''application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet'' });\n  }\n\n  function dataURLToBlob(dataURL) {\n    const [header, payload] = String(dataURL).split('','');\n    const mime = (/data:([^;]+)/.exec(header) || [])[1] || ''image/png'';\n    const binary = atob(payload || '''');\n    const bytes = new Uint8Array(binary.length);\n    for (let i = 0; i < binary.length; i++) bytes[i] = binary.charCodeAt(i);\n    return new Blob([bytes], { type: mime });\n  }\n\n  /** Hands the file to the host''s download service when it has one, else downloads it in the browser. */\n  async function deliverFile(blob, fileName, utilities) {\n    const hostExport = utilities && utilities.export;\n    if (hostExport && typeof hostExport.DownloadFile === ''function'') {\n      await hostExport.DownloadFile(blob, fileName);\n      return;\n    }\n    const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);\n    const anchor = document.createElement(''a'');\n    anchor.href = url;\n    anchor.download = fileName;\n    anchor.style.display = ''none'';\n    document.body.appendChild(anchor);\n    anchor.click();\n    anchor.remove();\n    setTimeout(() => URL.revokeObjectURL(url), 1000);\n  }\n\n  /** A CSS colour as [r, g, b, a] (0-255), read back from a 1x1 canvas so every colour syntax resolves; transparent when it cannot. */\n  function resolveCssColor(css) {\n    const canvas = document.createElement(''canvas'');\n    canvas.width = 1;\n    canvas.height = 1;\n    const context = canvas.getContext(''2d'');\n    if (!context) return [0, 0, 0, 0];\n    context.fillStyle = ''transparent'';\n    context.fillStyle = css;\n    context.fillRect(0, 0, 1, 1);\n    return Array.from(context.getImageData(0, 0, 1, 1).data);\n  }\n\n  /**\n   * The background the element is seen on, as a CSS colour: the nearest opaque background-color from\n   * the element up, with translucent ones blended over it, else white. In a dark theme a chart''s text\n   * is light, so an image drawn on white would lose its labels and legend.\n   */\n  function exportBackground(element) {\n    const layers = [];\n    for (let node = element; node; node = node.parentElement) {\n      const layer = resolveCssColor(getComputedStyle(node).backgroundColor);\n      if (layer[3] > 0) layers.push(layer);\n      if (layer[3] >= 255) break;\n    }\n    const [r, g, b] = layers.reduceRight((behind, layer) => {\n      const alpha = layer[3] / 255;\n      return behind.map((channel, i) => Math.round(layer[i] * alpha + channel * (1 - alpha)));\n    }, [255, 255, 255]);\n    return `rgb($' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{r}, $' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{g}, $' || CAST('' AS TEXT) || '{b})`;\n  }\n\n  /** Draws a PNG data URL onto `background`, so a transparent chart canvas keeps the colour it is shown on